1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,470
Up to four billion people rely
on herbal medicine.

2
00:00:03,740 --> 00:00:06,230
Most medicinal plants in trade are
taken from the wild.

3
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:08,410
And almost no one is
counting what is left.

4
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:13,000
In the Philippines, forests lose about one
hundred seventy-five hectares of tree

5
00:00:13,020 --> 00:00:14,130
cover every day.

6
00:00:14,220 --> 00:00:15,710
But nobody measures harvest.

7
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:17,740
So where should a ranger, or a harvester,

8
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:18,530
even start?

9
00:00:19,560 --> 00:00:20,530
Take tongkat ali.

10
00:00:20,780 --> 00:00:24,310
Its roots are dug up and sold, and it
is officially endangered.

11
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:28,630
Yet open databases hold only five
reliable records of it.

12
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:31,430
This is Harvester Risk Atlas:

13
00:00:31,720 --> 00:00:36,560
a free map that ranks which wild medicinal
plants of the Philippines need help

14
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:39,690
first, where they grow, and
how sure we are.

15
00:00:40,580 --> 00:00:42,110
It asks three questions.

16
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:43,530
How rare is it?

17
00:00:43,960 --> 00:00:45,210
Does harvesting kill it?

18
00:00:45,280 --> 00:00:46,450
Is its home disappearing?

19
00:00:46,900 --> 00:00:50,250
It measures rarity only where
botanists have searched well,

20
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:52,340
and re-runs the ranking two
thousand times,

21
00:00:52,360 --> 00:00:54,830
so every plant gets a range,
not one number.

22
00:00:55,580 --> 00:00:59,200
Pick tongkat ali on the atlas, and
you see broad hexagons,

23
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:00,330
never exact spots.

24
00:01:02,820 --> 00:01:05,250
Its profile explains why it ranks
second of twenty-nine,

25
00:01:05,640 --> 00:01:06,970
anywhere from first to fifth.

26
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:11,590
One click gives a ranger a
one-page field brief.

27
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:15,690
Move a slider, and the ranking changes.

28
00:01:18,180 --> 00:01:19,250
Now, what is new.

29
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:21,750
Every name is checked against
World Flora Online,

30
00:01:21,960 --> 00:01:24,950
and every plant against Co's Digital
Flora of the Philippines,

31
00:01:25,280 --> 00:01:28,030
built on the work of botanist Leonardo Co.

32
00:01:28,520 --> 00:01:30,500
For tongkat ali, Co's Flora agrees:

33
00:01:30,520 --> 00:01:32,290
native, and endangered.

34
00:01:32,560 --> 00:01:36,550
But it also records it on
Mindoro, Homonhon,

35
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:39,510
and Bucas Grande, where open
data has nothing.

36
00:01:39,900 --> 00:01:41,310
That is where to look next.

37
00:01:43,600 --> 00:01:47,840
Across all twenty-nine ranked plants, two thirds
of the land next to known records

38
00:01:47,860 --> 00:01:49,090
has barely been searched.

39
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:52,030
The atlas turns a ranking
into a survey plan.

40
00:01:53,080 --> 00:01:55,030
Five plants need attention now:

41
00:01:55,360 --> 00:02:00,230
kalingag, tongkat ali, bitaog,
tibig, and amuyon.

42
00:02:00,660 --> 00:02:02,990
Change the math, and the
order barely moves.

43
00:02:03,440 --> 00:02:07,890
It is not medical advice, and its harvest data
still needs experts in the field.

44
00:02:08,960 --> 00:02:10,190
Harvester Risk Atlas.

45
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:13,770
Which plants first, where to look
next, and how sure.

46
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:16,130
Help us protect the plants that heal us.

